WEBVTT

00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:07.180
These are grains of sand,
and each orange line

00:00:07.180 --> 00:00:09.348
joins two of them that
are pressing together.

00:00:09.698 --> 00:00:12.178
The thicker the line,
the harder they press,

00:00:12.178 --> 00:00:15.038
and together they hold
up this little house.

00:00:15.388 --> 00:00:18.128
Every gap between the
grains is full of water.

00:00:18.478 --> 00:00:22.008
Now shake the box, the way an
earthquake shakes wet ground.

00:00:22.358 --> 00:00:25.283
No grain breaks, and
nothing gets slippery.

00:00:25.633 --> 00:00:27.352
The house goes down anyway.

00:00:27.702 --> 00:00:29.141
So what let go?

00:00:30.991 --> 00:00:33.871
You already know three kinds
of sand from the beach.

00:00:34.221 --> 00:00:37.561
Dry sand pours, and the steepest
heap you can make settles

00:00:37.561 --> 00:00:40.258
at about thirty-four degrees,
however you pour it.

00:00:40.608 --> 00:00:44.148
Damp sand, packed into a
bucket and turned out, stands

00:00:44.148 --> 00:00:46.691
as a castle with walls
that go straight up.

00:00:47.041 --> 00:00:51.561
And at the water's edge, where every
gap is full of water, the sand feels

00:00:51.561 --> 00:00:55.772
firm while you stand still, then
swallows your feet when you wiggle them.

00:00:56.122 --> 00:00:58.491
It's the same sand every time.

00:00:58.841 --> 00:01:01.348
Only the water between the grains changes.

00:01:01.698 --> 00:01:07.271
An earth dam is a huge heap of sand and
soil, with a lake soaking into one side.

00:01:07.621 --> 00:01:11.058
Which of these three it acts
like decides whether it stands.

00:01:11.408 --> 00:01:15.216
So how can water make the same
sand stronger, and then weaker?

00:01:16.466 --> 00:01:20.320
The first answer most people reach
for is that water is slippery.

00:01:20.670 --> 00:01:25.454
Wet the grains and they slide past each
other, the way a wet floor makes you slip.

00:01:25.804 --> 00:01:27.522
The castle says otherwise.

00:01:27.872 --> 00:01:31.773
It has water in it, and it's
the strongest of the three.

00:01:32.123 --> 00:01:34.352
Sand grains are mostly quartz.

00:01:34.702 --> 00:01:38.082
When engineers slid polished
quartz against itself, wet

00:01:38.082 --> 00:01:41.111
surfaces gripped more than
twice as hard as dry ones.

00:01:41.461 --> 00:01:45.733
And on real, rough sand grains,
water barely changes the grip at all.

00:01:46.083 --> 00:01:49.938
So whatever water does to sand,
it isn't greasing the grains.

00:01:50.288 --> 00:01:54.003
To find out what it does, start
with something simpler than sand.

00:01:55.253 --> 00:01:58.550
Put a block on a plank
and slowly tilt the plank.

00:01:58.900 --> 00:02:03.498
The block stays put until the slope
gets steep, and then it slides.

00:02:03.848 --> 00:02:06.448
Friction holds it, and
friction comes from pressing.

00:02:06.798 --> 00:02:09.518
Press the block onto
the plank twice as hard,

00:02:09.518 --> 00:02:11.628
and it takes twice the push to move it.

00:02:11.978 --> 00:02:15.879
The grip on offer is always
the same fraction of the press.

00:02:16.229 --> 00:02:19.489
That fraction sets a
steepest angle: tilt past

00:02:19.489 --> 00:02:22.313
it and the block slides,
whatever it weighs.

00:02:22.663 --> 00:02:26.023
A heavier block presses
harder, but gravity also pulls

00:02:26.023 --> 00:02:28.328
it harder down the slope,
by the same share.

00:02:28.678 --> 00:02:33.276
For sand, that steepest angle is the
thirty-four degrees of the dry heap.

00:02:33.626 --> 00:02:35.948
Now tilt our ramp to twenty-five degrees.

00:02:36.298 --> 00:02:38.573
Two things are pulling
against each other here.

00:02:38.923 --> 00:02:43.149
Gravity pulls the block down the
slope, and friction grips back.

00:02:43.499 --> 00:02:48.468
The grip on offer is about one and a half
times what the block needs to stay put.

00:02:48.818 --> 00:02:51.744
That ratio is called the factor of safety.

00:02:52.094 --> 00:02:56.088
Above one, the block holds,
and below one, it slides.

00:02:57.338 --> 00:03:00.278
Now slip a thin cushion
of water under the block,

00:03:00.278 --> 00:03:02.957
and slowly pump up the pressure inside it.

00:03:03.307 --> 00:03:06.558
The block and the ramp
stay exactly as they were.

00:03:06.908 --> 00:03:10.948
Does the block hold, slide,
or hold even better?

00:03:13.798 --> 00:03:14.820
It slides.

00:03:15.170 --> 00:03:19.071
The water pushes up on the block, so
it carries part of the block's weight.

00:03:19.421 --> 00:03:23.693
Every bit of weight the water carries
comes off the block's press on the ramp.

00:03:24.043 --> 00:03:29.105
The grip shrinks with the press, while
gravity pulls down the slope as hard as ever.

00:03:29.455 --> 00:03:33.031
The block started with about
half again the grip it needed.

00:03:33.381 --> 00:03:36.681
So once the water carries just
under a third of the press,

00:03:36.681 --> 00:03:39.790
there's no spare grip
left, and the block goes.

00:03:40.140 --> 00:03:42.276
The block and the ramp never changed.

00:03:43.626 --> 00:03:46.552
Now shrink the block
down to a grain of sand.

00:03:46.902 --> 00:03:50.822
A handful of sand is a crowd
of grains, each resting

00:03:50.822 --> 00:03:53.403
on its neighbours at a
few points of contact.

00:03:53.753 --> 00:03:57.913
Every contact is a tiny block on
a tiny ramp, held by friction,

00:03:57.913 --> 00:04:01.230
and that friction comes from
how hard the two grains press.

00:04:01.580 --> 00:04:04.940
Locked together, so that to
move they have to climb over

00:04:04.940 --> 00:04:07.896
each other, those contacts
give the sand its strength.

00:04:08.246 --> 00:04:12.844
When the gaps are full of water, the water
in them pushes neighbouring grains apart.

00:04:13.194 --> 00:04:15.614
Like the cushion, it holds
up part of the weight

00:04:15.614 --> 00:04:18.534
above, so the grains
press on each other less.

00:04:18.884 --> 00:04:23.044
About a century ago, an engineer
named Karl Terzaghi put

00:04:23.044 --> 00:04:26.222
this into one rule, and it
founded soil mechanics.

00:04:26.572 --> 00:04:31.030
The strength of soil follows only the
press left over between the grains.

00:04:31.380 --> 00:04:36.442
That press is the weight of everything
above, minus the water pressure in the gaps.

00:04:36.792 --> 00:04:39.625
Engineers call it the effective stress.

00:04:40.875 --> 00:04:43.197
Here's what that rule does in real ground.

00:04:43.547 --> 00:04:46.054
Take a column of sand ten metres deep.

00:04:46.404 --> 00:04:50.909
The water table is the level below
which every gap is full of water.

00:04:51.259 --> 00:04:54.789
Keep it low, and the sand
above it is only damp.

00:04:55.139 --> 00:04:57.499
Then the grains at the
bottom press on each other

00:04:57.499 --> 00:05:00.944
with about eighteen tonnes
on every square metre.

00:05:01.294 --> 00:05:05.009
Now raise the water table
all the way to the surface.

00:05:05.359 --> 00:05:08.579
Soaking makes the sand heavier,
by about two tonnes on

00:05:08.579 --> 00:05:11.721
every square metre, because
water now fills the gaps.

00:05:12.071 --> 00:05:15.972
But the water in those gaps
pushes back up with about ten.

00:05:16.322 --> 00:05:19.712
So the grains now press
with only about ten tonnes.

00:05:20.062 --> 00:05:22.142
More than forty percent
of the sand's strength

00:05:22.142 --> 00:05:25.124
is gone, and not one grain had to move.

00:05:25.474 --> 00:05:29.375
Keep raising the water, above the
ground, like a lake sitting on top.

00:05:29.725 --> 00:05:34.926
The lake presses down from above, and pushes
up inside every gap by the same amount.

00:05:35.276 --> 00:05:38.434
So the press between the
grains doesn't change at all.

00:05:38.784 --> 00:05:41.617
So here is what water does to sand.

00:05:41.967 --> 00:05:45.357
It takes weight off the grains,
and their grip goes with it.

00:05:48.207 --> 00:05:50.761
That explains why soaked sand is weaker.

00:05:51.111 --> 00:05:53.851
The castle needs one more piece.

00:05:54.201 --> 00:05:57.301
Our friction rule said nothing
stands steeper than thirty-four

00:05:57.301 --> 00:06:00.238
degrees, yet the castle's
walls go straight up.

00:06:00.588 --> 00:06:03.688
In damp sand there's only
a little water, and it

00:06:03.688 --> 00:06:06.393
gathers in tiny rings
where the grains touch.

00:06:06.743 --> 00:06:10.743
The surface of each ring is
stretched, like the skin of a soap

00:06:10.743 --> 00:06:14.174
bubble, and it pulls the two
grains toward each other.

00:06:14.524 --> 00:06:18.889
The water in those rings is at less
than the pressure of the air around it.

00:06:19.239 --> 00:06:20.447
That's called suction.

00:06:20.797 --> 00:06:25.069
So this water pulls the grains together,
and the press between them goes up.

00:06:25.419 --> 00:06:27.959
That press doesn't come
from weight, so the

00:06:27.959 --> 00:06:30.202
thirty-four degree limit no longer holds.

00:06:30.552 --> 00:06:34.453
The pull is small, a few hundred
kilograms on a square metre.

00:06:34.803 --> 00:06:38.703
Even at the bottom of a thirty-centimetre
castle, the grains press with

00:06:38.703 --> 00:06:42.327
only about half a tonne, and near
the top, with almost nothing.

00:06:42.677 --> 00:06:47.088
So up there, the pull is most of
what holds the grains together.

00:06:47.438 --> 00:06:52.407
Here's the castle and the dry heap again,
in a simple model of grains, drawn flat.

00:06:52.757 --> 00:06:55.823
Cut a wall in damp grains, and it stands.

00:06:56.173 --> 00:06:59.377
Cut it in dry grains, and
it slumps into a heap.

00:06:59.727 --> 00:07:02.374
Dry the sand, and the rings evaporate.

00:07:02.724 --> 00:07:05.371
Flood it, and they merge
into ordinary water.

00:07:05.721 --> 00:07:07.671
Either way, the castle falls.

00:07:08.921 --> 00:07:13.240
That leaves the sand at the water's
edge, and the box we started with.

00:07:13.590 --> 00:07:17.070
The grains in our box are
loosely packed, with big holes

00:07:17.070 --> 00:07:20.046
between them, and every
hole is full of water.

00:07:20.396 --> 00:07:23.516
Each shake shoves the loose
grains, and they try to settle

00:07:23.516 --> 00:07:26.572
into the holes below them,
like a shaken jar of rice.

00:07:26.922 --> 00:07:30.451
But the water is in the way, and
it can't get out fast enough.

00:07:30.801 --> 00:07:35.167
Trapped, its pressure climbs, until
it nearly matches the weight above.

00:07:35.517 --> 00:07:39.557
For a moment, the water holds up
the house, and the grains don't.

00:07:39.907 --> 00:07:42.927
Weight minus water pressure
is almost nothing, so

00:07:42.927 --> 00:07:45.201
the grains hardly press
on each other at all.

00:07:45.551 --> 00:07:48.802
The orange lines fade, and
the grip goes with them.

00:07:49.152 --> 00:07:51.195
The house tips and sinks.

00:07:51.545 --> 00:07:52.985
That is liquefaction.

00:07:53.335 --> 00:07:56.772
Now the same shaking, on
densely packed grains.

00:07:57.122 --> 00:08:00.402
These grains have no holes
to drop into; to move

00:08:00.402 --> 00:08:03.066
at all, they have to
ride up over each other.

00:08:03.416 --> 00:08:08.385
That opens the gaps, and bigger gaps need
more water than can flow in so quickly.

00:08:08.735 --> 00:08:13.704
The water is pulled thin, a pull like
the castle's, and the grains grip harder.

00:08:14.054 --> 00:08:16.562
The house sinks only a fraction as far.

00:08:16.912 --> 00:08:21.277
Loose, soaked sand like this is what
swallows your feet when you wiggle them.

00:08:21.627 --> 00:08:25.807
Stand still, and the water seeps
away, and the grains press again.

00:08:27.057 --> 00:08:29.750
So that's the idea under
everything that follows.

00:08:30.100 --> 00:08:33.723
Sand and soil are strong because
their grains press on each other.

00:08:34.073 --> 00:08:36.213
Water pressure in the
gaps takes some of that

00:08:36.213 --> 00:08:39.135
press away, and suction can add some back.

00:08:39.485 --> 00:08:43.896
Every dam in this series stands
or falls on that one idea.

00:08:45.246 --> 00:08:49.147
If water pressure steals strength,
how much of it does a lake bring?

00:08:49.497 --> 00:08:52.980
Say two dams each hold back
water fifty metres deep.

00:08:53.330 --> 00:08:55.792
One holds a lake ten kilometres long.

00:08:56.142 --> 00:09:00.182
The other holds a slot two metres
wide, with solid rock behind it.

00:09:00.532 --> 00:09:02.529
Which dam has to be stronger?
